-- title:
--   Enlarging Lemma, pp.18, 33 (corrected: $d\ge 3$)
-- statement:
--   Let $\mathcal H$ have dimension $d\ge 3$ and let $|i\rangle,|j\rangle$ be orthonormal. Let $\mathcal H_1=\mathfrak{su}(\tilde{\mathcal H})$ on the subspace $\tilde{\mathcal H}=|i\rangle^\perp$ "that does not contain $|i\rangle$", and $\mathcal H_2=\mathfrak{su}(\operatorname{span}\{|i\rangle,|j\rangle\})$, both extended by zero. Then $[\mathcal H_1,\mathcal H_2]$ (all commutators) generates $\mathfrak{su}(d)$ on $\mathcal H$ as a real Lie algebra: the real Lie algebra generated by the $[ia,ib]$, $a\in\mathcal H_1$, $b\in\mathcal H_2$, consists exactly of the $iA$ with $A$ Hermitian and traceless.
--
--   It closes the special case $J=n/2-1$ of the proof of Theorem 5.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - correction: any dimension d → d ≥ 3; reason: at d = 2, H̃ is one-dimensional, su(1) = 0, so all commutators vanish and generate 0, not su(2)
--   - reading: a set H₁ that generates su(d − 1) on H̃, a set H₂ that generates su(2) on span{|i⟩, |j⟩} → H₁ = su(H̃) and H₂ = su(span{|i⟩, |j⟩}), the whole algebras; reason: the proof uses X_jk ∈ H₁ and Y_ij ∈ H₂; read as bare generating sets the claim fails at d = 3 (H₁ = {iX_jk, iY_jk}, H₂ = {iX_ij, iY_ij} give an algebra of dimension 3, not 8)
--   - encoding: |j⟩ another state in H → |i⟩, |j⟩ orthonormal; reason: the proof treats i, j, k as orthonormal basis states
--   - reading: generates su(d) on H under closure as a Lie algebra → the generated real Lie algebra equals {iA : A Hermitian, tr A = 0}; reason: as for the Mixing Lemma
-- source:
--   Kempe, Bacon, Lidar & Whaley, Theory of Decoherence-Free Fault-Tolerant Universal Quantum Computation, arXiv:quant-ph/0004064v2, Enlarging Lemma, §VI, p.18; Appendix C, p.33

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_CollectiveDFS_IsSuOn

namespace CollectiveDFS

attribute [local instance 100] LieRing.ofAssociativeRing

/-- The Enlarging Lemma (Kempe, Bacon, Lidar & Whaley, arXiv:quant-ph/0004064v2, §VI, p.18, and Appendix C, p.33, PDF pp.18, 33): let `H` have
dimension `d ≥ 3`, let `|i⟩, |j⟩` be orthonormal, let `H₁ = su(H̃)` on the subspace `H̃ = |i⟩^⊥`
"that does not contain |i⟩" and `H₂ = su(span{|i⟩, |j⟩})`, both extended by zero. Then
`[H₁, H₂]` (all commutators) generates `su(d)` on `H` as a real Lie algebra: the real Lie algebra
generated by the `[i a, i b]`, `a ∈ H₁`, `b ∈ H₂`, consists exactly of the `i A` with `A` Hermitian
and traceless.

**Formalization Note** `H = EuclideanSpace ℂ ι`, `d = card ι`. `[i a, i b] = i (i[a, b])` is the
anti-Hermitian form of the Hamiltonian commutator `i[a, b]`. Corrected: `d ≥ 3` (at `d = 2`,
`su(1) = 0` and all commutators vanish). Read: `H₁, H₂` are the whole algebras, as the proof uses
(as bare generating sets the claim fails at `d = 3`), and `|j⟩` is orthonormal to `|i⟩`. -/
theorem enlarging_lemma {ι : Type*} [Fintype ι] [DecidableEq ι] (hd : 3 ≤ Fintype.card ι)
    (vi vj : EuclideanSpace ℂ ι) (hon : Orthonormal ℂ ![vi, vj]) (A : Matrix ι ι ℂ) :
    Complex.I • A ∈ LieSubalgebra.lieSpan ℝ (Matrix ι ι ℂ)
        {M | ∃ a b : Matrix ι ι ℂ, IsSuOn (Submodule.span ℂ {vi})ᗮ a ∧
          IsSuOn (Submodule.span ℂ {vi, vj}) b ∧ M = ⁅Complex.I • a, Complex.I • b⁆} ↔
      A.IsHermitian ∧ A.trace = 0 := by sorry

end CollectiveDFS
